from research_schema import F

F('horse-trade','Horse trading and royal imagery','The Hampi royal reliefs include horses, handlers and military activity; the lecture explicitly places them in a trade discussion. Treat this as a connection between circulated animals and royal display. Individual handlers’ origins and the exact journey of each depicted horse remain unestablished.','A horse in a religious or decorative image is not automatically a record of a commercial transaction.','hampi-dibba asi-hampi')
F('belitung','Belitung shipwreck and maritime cargo','The Smithsonian exhibition introduces a cargo dominated by Chinese ceramics, documenting large-scale maritime exchange. A dated bowl helps contextualize the cargo but should not be treated as the exact date of every vessel or the sinking. Manufacture and destination are separate historical questions.','One dated object cannot independently date every cargo item, and a shipwreck is not evidence that all trade followed one route.','si-belitung')
F('maritime-networks','Maritime networks: goods, people and knowledge','The Belitung cargo and Indian textile histories provide material cases of long-distance circulation. The course extends this inquiry to makers, images and cultural practices. Connections need object-specific evidence; broad oceanic framing does not prove a complete itinerary for every artwork.','Trade networks existed before European companies; their later participation does not make them the origin of all Asian exchange.','si-belitung va-textiles')
F('voc','VOC and the distinction between collector and maker','European companies entered established textile networks. An object’s association with a Dutch warehouse, buyer or collection does not identify its maker or prove VOC ownership at every stage.','European collection history and Asian manufacture are compatible; neither should replace the other in an object record.','va-textiles')
F('trade-objects','Trade objects inside pictures','An imported vessel, carpet or textile inside a painting is evidence of pictorial selection and familiarity with goods. The Asia in Amsterdam exhibition connected Asian luxury imports with Dutch art and consumption. Exact pictured-object provenance still needs its own identification.','A painted object is not the physical museum object placed beside it, even when their forms closely resemble one another.','rijks-asia met-carpets')
F('collecting','Collecting and the changing life of objects','Collections assemble objects into new relationships. The Asia in Amsterdam exhibition connected imported goods and images of consumption, while the lecture’s albums and textile displays show other acts of selection. Production, acquisition, mounting and exhibition need separate records.','Present museum location does not establish original destination, owner, ritual use or commission.','rijks-asia')
F('museum-display','Exhibition display as an argument','Physical proximity, height, framing, barriers and sightlines affect what viewers compare. The Asia in Amsterdam project explicitly connected Asian goods and Dutch art; the lecture photographs show how particular arrangements realize such comparisons. Spatial description is the evidence for an interpretation of the display.','A photograph of objects together is not proof that they were originally made or used together.','rijks-asia')
F('biennale','Biennale as a curatorial and urban setting','The Kochi-Muziris catalogue documents site-specific work installed in Aspinwall House. The lecture adds street posters and other environments, connecting contemporary exhibition to local public space and longer maritime histories. These are distinct components of the event rather than one undifferentiated artwork.','A biennale’s date is not the date of every historical object it exhibits or every material it reuses.','tallur-biennale')
F('ocean-contemporary','The ocean in contemporary exhibition practice','The lecture places ships, maps, paper boats, shore imagery and Kerkar’s encrusted cross within an inquiry into present oceanic connections. Their actual arrangement and captions support the comparison. The search did not establish a single artist-endorsed meaning shared by every linked work.','Marine material or a boat form alone does not establish one migration narrative, immersion duration or intended political statement.',search='research14 research22',status='draft')
F('connected-histories','Connected histories as the course method','The course reads art through movement among places, communities and media. Textile trade, Persianate court culture and imported print adaptation supply documented examples. The methodological claim is to examine evidenced relationships while retaining different regional histories; it is not a claim that all similarities share one cause.','Connectedness is not sameness, an uninterrupted route, or a substitute for proving the particular relation under discussion.','va-textiles met-deccan',aliases='oceanic connections|cultural amalgamation')
F('craft-agency','Craft agency and the distribution of credit','Print production, dye work and botanical illustration involve distinct skilled roles. The source records identify collaborative makers or Indian artists associated with named commissioners. The course’s social dimension asks whose labour becomes visible and whose name is retained in conventional attribution.','A patron, designer, publisher or commissioner is not automatically the sole physical maker.','met-ukiyoe met-dyes rbge-roxburgh')
F('conceptual-authorship','Conceptual authorship and execution','LeWitt’s account separates an artwork’s prior conception from its execution. This supplies a framework for examining how an idea, fabrication and presentation can involve different roles. It does not erase craft labour or establish who conceived an anonymous work in a slide.','Conceptual authorship is not the claim that making is irrelevant or that any unlabelled installation can be attributed to a famous conceptual artist.','moma-lewitt')
F('colonial-documentation','Colonial documentation and selective visibility','Travel imagery, botanical projects and photographs record places and people within particular commissions and markets. Roxburgh’s drawings and Beato’s photographs show why image-maker credit and production circumstances matter. The course interprets documentation as evidence that requires framing, rather than a complete neutral record.','Documentation can preserve information while also selecting and staging it; these possibilities are compatible.','rbge-roxburgh ludwig-beato')
F('cartography','Maps and globes: knowledge, representation and power','The course uses route maps, urban plans, a geographer’s interior and Mughal globe allegories for different arguments. Scholarship on Jahangir establishes the imperial use of cartographic form in a specific case. Distinguish practical mapping, retrospective representation and symbolic possession.','A globe in a study and an emperor standing on a globe do not make the same claim; map date and city foundation date also differ.','whc-walrus')
F('picturesque','The picturesque and represented travel','The UCSB exhibition connects travel books and an Indian picturesque associated with Hodges and the Daniells. Framing monuments and terrain as composed views shapes how distant readers encounter a place. A dramatic viewpoint is a pictorial decision, even when the monument itself is real.','A picturesque image is not a measured survey; a print’s publication date need not be the date of the artist’s visit.','ucsb-picturesque bl-daniell')
F('orientalism-occidentalism','Orientalism and the course’s Occidentalism comparison','The Met account describes Western constructions of an imagined East. The lecture contrasts this with images and adaptations involving European subjects in Asian settings. The paired course usage should be retained, but the research pass did not establish a universally agreed, symmetrical definition of Occidentalism.','Reversing the direction of looking does not automatically reverse unequal power relations or produce an equivalent historical theory.','met-orientalism',search='research13',status='draft')
F('popular-print','Popular prints and wider circulation','Print processes enabled multiple impressions and wider image circulation. The Calcutta Kali/Tara diptych is a documented colour-print case; the lecture includes devotional, commercial and social subjects. Audience, price and edition size cannot all be inferred from subject or reproducibility alone.','Popular subject matter does not mean anonymous authorship, identical editions or one uniform audience.','sas-colour met-kali-tara')
F('mass-production','Reproducibility and production scale','The Belitung cargo documents large-scale ceramic output, while print technology enables repeated impressions from prepared matrices. These are different manufacturing systems. Repetition in a design alone does not establish industrial machinery or the exact number produced.','Reproducible, mass-produced and mechanically identical are not interchangeable descriptions.','si-belitung sas-colour')
F('poster','Posters: publicity, image and placement','The lecture shows entertainment advertisements and contemporary exhibition publicity, including repeated designs on Kochi walls. Lettering, image hierarchy, repeated display and physical placement are observable. The particular printer, paper, edition size and production machinery are generally not supplied.','A photographed poster remains distinct from the photographer’s image; advertising subject matter does not identify a specific print process.',search='research24 research25',status='draft')
